# ⚠⚠ THIS FIXTURE'S SIGSEGV IS A COMPILER BUG, AND ONLY THE SELF-HOST LANE
# STILL HAS IT. READ BEFORE "FIXING" A RED HERE.
#
# The wired test (`sh_gg_run_propagates_signal_death`) asserts exit == 139 and
# "segmentation" in stderr from the SELF-HOST driver. The SIGSEGV is NOT
# scaffolding and NOT intentional: it is a comprehension-emitter defect where
# the result-push uses the SOURCE element size instead of the RESULT element
# size, so a type-changing comprehension copies the wrong width out of an
# 8-byte stack temp.
#
# LANE STATUS (R44):
#   • RUST gg — FIXED. The emitters now defer the accumulator mint until the
#     loop body has produced a MATERIALIZED element, so the slot is minted from
#     the result element type. `gg run` on this program is rc 0 printing `1`,
#     and `gg build --sanitize` on it is CLEAN (measured: zero ASan/Leak lines,
#     with an unbounded-recursion control still reporting `stack-overflow` on
#     the same build). Its `tests/sanitize/CORRUPTION_ALLOWLIST.txt` row
#     retired WITH that fix, on its own stated terms.
#   • SELF-HOST — STILL BROKEN, which is why this test is still green. The SH
#     list arm mints from `collection_element_type(coll_tn)`, i.e. the SOURCE
#     collection's element name. That is routed to R44 Track K.
#
# ⚠ TWO FIGURES, TWO SHAPES — do not merge them. Measured at HEAD under
# `gg build --sanitize`, both `ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow`:
#   • THIS fixture's own nested program below
#     (`Vector[Vector[int]] -> Vector[int]`)          => READ of size 64
#   • the `Vector[String] -> Vector[int]` minimal form => READ of size 32
#
#         void main():
#             Vector[String] words = ["ab", "cde"]
#             Vector[int] r = [w.len() for w in words]   # SH: SIGSEGV, rc 139
#             print(r.get(0).unwrap())
#
# The longhand `for` loop with `.push()` is clean on both lanes.
#
# ⛔ THE "RE-POINT THIS AT A DELIBERATE NULL DEREF" ORDER IS RETRACTED (R44).
# It existed for a Rust-lane oracle (`gg_run_propagates_signal_death`) that has
# since been re-pointed at `tests/fixtures/stack_guard_deep_recursion.gg` — an
# intentional overflow that `tests/security.rs` ratifies — precisely so no
# oracle rides on a defect under repair. THIS test needs no re-point: its
# oracle is the SELF-HOST lane, whose defect is still live and filed. When
# Track K lands, this test goes red, and THAT red means the fix worked; retire
# the test then, do not weaken the assertion and do not "fix" the
# comprehension back.
#
# (Found 2026-08-18 by the corpus-wide ASan census, which is also how the
# mechanism — wrong elem_size at the push, a write-site defect — was identified.)
#
# Regression (Round XXV Track A subsumed at scout stage 2026-08-02):
# SH driver's `gg run` already propagates signal-death — verified live.
#
# The Round XXIV Track B follow-up filed this as a defect (SH exits 255 silent
# on SIGSEGV), but the filing was source-read (Core #12 / #15d violation):
# the scout inspected `gorget_exec` and saw the `-1 → exit(-1) → 255` branch
# but never actually built + ran. That branch fires only if the SHELL ITSELF
# is signal-killed, never for a signal-killed user program.
#
# MEASURED at HEAD: `gorget_exec` uses `system()`, which invokes `/bin/sh -c
# <cmd>`. When the child dies from SIGSEGV, /bin/sh translates the signal to
# 128+signo = 139 (with a "Segmentation fault" stderr line) before exiting;
# `system()` reports WIFEXITED=true, WEXITSTATUS=139. `gorget_exec` returns
# 139. `exit(139)` gives 139. Both lanes are UX-distinguishable from a
# compile error.
#
# Difference (nice-to-have, NOT a defect):
#   • Rust `gg run` emits BRANDED stderr: `gg: <exe> terminated by SIGSEGV
#     (signal 11)` (via `propagate_child_status` reading ExitStatusExt::signal).
#   • SH driver emits SHELL stderr: `Segmentation fault` (via /bin/sh's own
#     signal-translation on system()).
#
# Both are non-empty stderr + exit 139. Getting branded parity requires a
# fork/execvp-based new runtime API (system() can't distinguish "shell
# exited 139 because child SEGV'd" from "child intentionally exited 139").
# Cost: medium; value: cosmetic. Not filed unless asked.
#
# This fixture is a LIVE regression pin: SH `driver run` propagates the
# child's exit code (139) with signal-indicating stderr. Also a worked
# example of Core #15d MIND-THE-PROBE (source-reading vs build-and-run) and
# Core #15e Q1 (asymmetry vs ratified — verify before filing).
#
# ⚠ WHY A LIVE PIN LIVES IN `known_gaps/`. `scripts/convergence.sh` ratifies
# exactly this placement — "NOT A GAP AT ALL = a known_gaps fixture referenced
# ONLY by LIVE tests … parked in that directory because `runtime_parity_corpus`
# never descends subdirectories". Two top-level scanners would otherwise pick
# this up and get the wrong answer:
#   • `runtime_parity_corpus` (a top-level `read_dir`) stdout-diffs Rust gg
#     against the self-host on LANGUAGE-SEMANTICS parity. This fixture tests a
#     `gg run` DRIVER property, not semantics, and its Rust half now passes
#     while the SH half still SIGSEGVs — so it would be a permanent counted
#     CRASH row for a defect already pinned by the test above.
#   • `scripts/sanitize_sweep.sh` (`find tests/fixtures -maxdepth 1`)
#     sanitizes the RUST lane, which no longer corrupts here.
# Do NOT move it back to the top level.

int seed():
    return 1

void main():
    Vector[int] inner0 = [seed()]
    Vector[int] inner1 = [seed() + 1]
    Vector[Vector[int]] a = [inner0, inner1]
    Vector[int] r = [x.len() for x in a]
    print(r.get(0).unwrap())
